// expect-ok
// Z3 corpus #11: axis-1 — time-bound approval, expressible today.
//
// The fixture #10 deferred-work list flagged "time-bound approvals"
// as a real gap. Examining what "time-bound" actually means revealed
// it's expressible with existing primitives:
//
//   * Caps stay caps (linear, unforgeable).
//   * The DEADLINE is a regular `i64` constant or runtime parameter.
//   * `time::now_ms()` is a runtime-side check.
//   * A handler function pattern-matches: if `now_ms() < deadline_ms`
//     then use the cap, else don't (the cap is consumed either way
//     because linear values can't be returned unused).
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE PROVES
//
// A `gated_use` function takes an approval cap and a deadline. If
// the wall clock is before the deadline, the cap is used (passed to
// a sink). If past the deadline, the cap is consumed but the action
// doesn't fire — the policy author's "valid until T" intent is
// honored at runtime without requiring any new language primitive.
//
// This is the WEAKER form of time-bound: the cap itself doesn't
// carry the deadline in its type. The deadline is metadata the user
// threads through. A STRONGER form (where the cap's type carries the
// deadline and a use-after-deadline is statically impossible) would
// require deadline-typed caps — a real new primitive, deferred and
// regression-locked in fixture `14_deadline_typed_cap_wall.sigil`
// (step 33, axis-1 sixth touch). That fixture documents the parser-
// level wall and gives acceptance criteria for the closing iteration.
//
// Today's encoding has one caveat: linear consumption is unconditional.
// If `now_ms() >= deadline_ms`, the cap is still consumed (the
// function takes ownership and discards). Callers must understand
// that "passing a cap past its deadline" wastes the cap rather than
// preserving it. A future iteration could add `Result<T, Cap>` to
// return-and-recover, but that needs its own design.
//
// REQUIREMENTS
//
// The fixture is an outer-ring trusted module because time::now_ms()
// is FFI-backed (TimeGrant::Wall required at runtime).
#[ring(outer)] #[trusted]
module ext;

// Wall-clock FFI shim (mirrors sigil::time::time_now). Inlined here
// because the corpus harness compiles each fixture standalone without
// the stdlib search path.
extern "C" fn time_now() -> i64 ! { FFI, Unsafe };

// `valid_until_ms` is the deadline; `now` is the wall clock at use.
// Returns 1 if the action would fire (deadline future), 0 if past.
// In a real policy the `return 1` branch would invoke the privileged
// operation; this fixture demonstrates the DECISION shape that
// requires no new language primitive.
// Return type is @Internal because `time_now()` is FFI and FFI returns
// are tainted @Internal — even branching on the result poisons the
// return taint. A real policy that needs a @Public yes/no would
// declassify via a Declassify cap; that's a separate cap operation
// orthogonal to time-bound semantics.
fn gated_use(valid_until_ms: i64) -> i64 @Internal ! { FFI, Unsafe } {
    let now: i64 @Internal = time_now();
    if now < valid_until_ms {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return 0;
    }
}
